-- FINDING 7 (silent; read as a defect in the unstable build, shelf and dev alike, for the language designers to confirm).  The pin is documented as deep, and a value
-- behind JUST, LIST or a record field IS pinned when the field is a named rule.  But a time built-in written
-- DIRECTLY as a constructor argument or record field (`Stamp WITH d IS TODAY`, `JUST TODAY`, `LIST TODAY`,
-- `Stamp WITH d IS RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`) escapes the pin and reads the ambient clock.
-- It is not one of the four documented boundaries of the deep pin (closures, obligations, the depth budget, the
-- back-edge arm: TEMPORAL-RULE-VERSION-DESIGN.md section 1.4.1), which is why it is read as a defect.
-- The first four directives print 3 Nov 2026 (the fixed clock) where 1 Aug 2012 or 1 Jun 2011 is meant; the next
-- three, with one name removed, print the pinned day; a bare scalar (the last) is pinned.
-- The row therefore never hands a bare time built-in to a constructor: sgbau-first-schedule.l4 names each in a
-- rule first.
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

DECLARE Stamp HAS d IS A DATE

`the day the law is read` MEANS TODAY
`the day whose text governs` MEANS `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`

#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 8 2012) (Stamp WITH d IS TODAY)
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2011) (Stamp WITH d IS `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 8 2012) (JUST TODAY)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 8 2012) (LIST TODAY)
-- the same values, one name removed: pinned
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 8 2012) (Stamp WITH d IS `the day the law is read`)
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2011) (Stamp WITH d IS `the day whose text governs`)
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 8 2012) (JUST `the day the law is read`)
-- a bare scalar is pinned
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 8 2012) TODAY
